{
  "requirement": "The source tools identify project files with project-relative `PathBuf` values.\n\n```rust,ignore\nlet file = std::path::PathBuf::from(\"src/auth.rs\");\n```\n\nAn item within that file uses the path and selector in `OutlineData` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.address_identity`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural clause is: \"The source tools identify project files with project-relative `PathBuf` values.\" The `OutlineData` path-and-selector shape is cited from `requirements.sdk.outlines.address_identity`, which another requirement implements. The roots carry the PathBuf obligation:\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[list]` returns `Vec<PathBuf>`. It maps the provider's resource locations with `PathBuf::from`. Returning `Vec<String>` instead would give the same text but violate the clause.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[read]` takes `impl AsRef<Path>` and converts it with `project_resource`. `parse` takes the same kind of path and delegates to `read`. A `String`-keyed API would break the clause.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#struct[TextMatch]` declares `pub path: PathBuf`. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#struct[StructuralMatch]` declares `node: OutlineData`, and `OutlineData` holds a `PathBuf` path.\n\nThe constructors `TextMatch::new` and `StructuralMatch::new` only forward their arguments into those marked fields. `search_text` and `search_structure` have no address type of their own. Their return types, `TextMatches` and `StructuralMatches`, are aliases of `SearchResults<TextMatch>` and `SearchResults<StructuralMatch>`. `search_text` delegates to the provider. `search_structure` delegates to the analysis. The address representation is fixed by the marked struct fields, so the search methods need no separate marker.\n\nThe \"project-relative\" wording is a behavioral property of the values the provider produces. Whether paths come out relative is a behavior question, not an additional structural obligation here.\n\nbehavioral: `requirements.sdk.sources.source_addresses` has two behavioral obligations. The reviewed tests cover both.\n\n**1. Source tools identify project files with project-relative `PathBuf` values.**\n- `gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository` checks that `list` returns relative paths such as `keep.txt` and `src/nested.txt`. Its `read`, `parse` and `search_text` calls take relative file paths. It also asserts that `search_text` match paths equal `Path::new(\"src/nested.txt\")`.\n- `scoped_inspection_skips_unrelated_directories_and_reports_relevant_ignore_errors` asserts that `listed[0]` equals `Path::new(\"src/one.txt\")`. That is a nested, project-relative path.\n- `empty_sources_errors_and_retained_snapshots_remain_distinct` checks that `parsed.id().path` is `note.txt`. It reads by a `PathBuf` and rejects `../` paths and absolute paths, so only project-relative addresses are accepted.\n- `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` asserts `found.matches[0].node.path == Path::new(\"visible.rs\")`. It makes the same check for `script.py`.\n- `local_provider_scopes_paths_and_rejects_resources_outside_its_root` covers the local provider's root-scoped resource locations and rejects outside resources.\n\n**2. An item within a file uses the `OutlineData` path and selector.**\n- `empty_sources_errors_and_retained_snapshots_remain_distinct` asserts that the file-level id has path `note.txt` and selector `\"\"`.\n- `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` asserts that the structural match node has path `visible.rs` and selector `fn[visible]`.\n- `outline_addresses_validate_selectors_without_reading_paths` and `addresses_preserve_opaque_fields_and_compare_each_identity_component` check path and selector preservation. The second also checks identity comparison on each field.\n\nThe identity and selector-validation rules belong to the cited `requirements.sdk.outlines.address_identity`, which is context only. I did not reassess its tests.\n\nI found no gap. All the reviewed tests already carry `{test}` markers for the subject.",
  "references": "53f623c21a8c8f4ae0b31fe206cfbef1b41519c0fed7517ec4486b38b8b92dfd",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.sdk.sources.source_addresses",
      "matches": [
